# MIDP2 Vendor-Specific Keymap Table Specification

SquirrelJME supports multiple vendor keycodes, which were used by their MIDP2
devices due to MIDP2's LCDUI not specifying keys other than the numpad and
basic game keys. For this reason, vendors differed on how they mapped those
non-standard keys in Java ME, although some specific devices also have a
different numpad keymap on top of those.

Vendor keycodes are defined in their respective `.csv` file.

Currently, the following vendor keymaps are supported by SquirrelJME:
 * `com.kddi`
 * `com.motorola.a1000`
 * `com.motorola` (also used by SoftBank and Alcatel devices)
 * `com.motorola.triplets` (also used by Sharp devices)
 * `com.motorola.v8` (also used by Sharp devices)
 * `com.nokia` (Also used by Samsung and Sony Ericsson)
 * `com.nokia.keyboard` (Nokia C/E-Series in numpad mode)
 * `com.sagem`
 * `com.siemens`
 * `kr.co.lge`
 * `net.rim.blackberry`

## Keymap File Structure (`.csv`)

All CSV files are comma-separated, containing keycode names and their
corresponding values on each line.

For values that a vendor does not define and/or the default assumed
mapping is enough, the value must be specified as zero to let SquirrelJME
use its own internal keymapping default. The mapping may potentially be
ignored if there is no default keymap available.

The ordering of key/value pairs in the CSV is not important. Any keys whose
value is unknown for that keymap, or is known to be the same value as the
default mapping for said key, shall be left as zero.

`haskeybaord` is a special key that indicates whether the device in question
has a keyboard, and said keyboard is to be set to ASCII input mode. As such,
not all keyboard mappings will have it set to `1`, as vendors such as
`nokia.keyboard` do indicate a keyboard device, but the keyboard in that case
is in numpad mode for games and apps that do not support the full keyboard.

Specific vendors such as Nokia, KDDI, and LG have different names for
special keys such as _center_ being named _soft3_ and _back_ being named _CLR_.
SquirrelJME aligns its key names as closely to the main Java ME LCDUI
specification as it can.


## Keymap File Example

This is an example of how valid keymap files are expected to be defined
(this table reflects `com.nokia.csv`):

| keycode      | value |
|--------------|-------|
| keyup        | 1     |
| keydown      | 6     |
| keyleft      | 2     |
| keyright     | 5     |
| keycenter    | -9    |
| keysoft1     | -6    |
| keysoft2     | -7    |
| keyback      | -88   |
| keynum0      | 0     |
| keynum1      | 0     |
| keynum2      | 0     |
| keynum3      | 0     |
| keynum4      | 0     |
| keynum5      | 0     |
| keynum6      | 0     |
| keynum7      | 0     |
| keynum8      | 0     |
| keynum9      | 0     |
| keystar      | 0     |
| keypound     | 0     |
| haskeyboard  | 0     |
